1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is the VOA Special English Technology Report.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
An American Space Company says a powerful new rocket should be ready for a test launch

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
by the end of next year.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
The company is Space Exploration Technologies or Space X.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Its new rocket is called the Falcon Heavy.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Company officials say it will be able to transport satellites or spacecraft,

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
weighing up to 53 metric tons into orbit.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
That load weight is double the capacity of NASA Space Shuttles.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The Space Agency is retiring its shuttles after 30 years.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Elon Musk is the Chief Executive Officer of SpaceX.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:02,000
He says 53 metric tons is more than the weight of a fully loaded Boeing 737,

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
with 136 passengers, luggage, and fuel.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:16,000
It's more payload capability than any space vehicle in history, he says, apart from the Saturn V.

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:25,000
NASA used Saturn V rockets during its Apollo and Skylab programs in the 1960s and 70s.

15
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:34,000
A Saturn V launched the Apollo 11 mission that landed the first humans on the moon in 1969.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The rockets were removed from service in 1973, but they remain the most powerful ever built.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Elon Musk says the Falcon Heavy will be the second most powerful rocket ever.

18
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:59,000
He says it was designed to do more than carry satellites and other equipment into space.

19
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:06,000
He says the rocket was designed to meet NASA's ratings for human flight safety.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,000
So it could someday be used to carry astronauts and other travelers into space.

21
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Mr. Musk says the Falcon Heavy could also be used for missions like carrying a robotic lander to collect samples from Mars.

22
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
He says a mission like that requires a great amount of lift capability.

23
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The lander has to reach Mars and still have enough fuel to return to Earth.

24
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The first launch is planned from the company's launch site at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California.

25
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:57,000
A launch from Cape Canaveral, Florida is expected in late 2013 or 2014.

26
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
In time, SpaceX hopes to launch 10 Falcon Heavy rockets a year.

27
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
It says the rocket should reduce launch costs to about $2,000 a kilogram.

28
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:17,000
That is about one-tenth the cost of carrying loads and orbit on a space shuttle.

29
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:30,000
SpaceX already has a billion-and-a-half dollar deal with NASA to use a smaller rocket to transport cargo to the International Space Station.

30
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:42,000
The rocket is the Falcon 9 and the deal is for after the two last shuttles, Endeavor and Atlantis are retired this year.

31
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
For VOA Special English, I'm Carolyn Prasuti.

32
00:03:46,000 --> 00:04:00,000
For more ways to learn American English and stay informed every day, go to VOASpecialEnglish.com from your computer or mobile device.

